@inject PreferenceStore Preferences

@* THE OWNER'S OWN SCORE, as twelve radios in a fieldset, and every part of that is a refusal of
   something easier.

   NOT type="range". A slider has no visible value of its own, its keyboard step is a browser decision,
   and on a touch screen a 1-in-11 target is a drag a reader has to land precisely -- SC 2.5.7 Dragging
   Movements asks for a single-pointer alternative, and the alternative IS this. NOT type="number"
   either: a spinner accepts "7.5" and "-3" and "banana", so the control would need validation, an error
   message and a live region to announce it, all to collect one of eleven values.

   A RADIO GROUP IS ONE TAB STOP. That is why the name attribute below is load-bearing rather than
   decoration: radios without a shared name are not a group, so each of the twelve would take focus on
   its own and this one control would add twelve stops to the page's tab order. Grouped, Tab reaches the
   group and the arrow keys move within it, which is what a reader who knows radios expects.

   "NOT RATED" IS A REAL OPTION AND IT COMES FIRST. Without it there is no way back: a reader who scored
   something by accident could change the score but never clear it, and a group where every option is
   taken is a group with no way out (the owner would have to delete site data). First because it is the
   state every title starts in, so the group reads from "nothing" upwards, and because the arrow keys
   wrap -- so it is one key from 10.

   THE LEGEND CARRIES THE SCALE AND THE OPTIONS CARRY ONLY THE NUMBER. A screen reader reads the legend
   with each option, so "Your rating out of 10" plus "7" is "Your rating out of 10, 7" -- said once per
   move -- where a label of "7 out of 10" would make it "Your rating out of 10, 7 out of 10". The legend
   is VISIBLE, unlike the facet panel's, because this group is not inside a disclosure whose summary
   already names it.

   INTEGERS ONLY, 0 to 10, which is TMDB's own scale and so the same units as the score on the tile. Half
   points would double the group and invite a comparison the owner never asked for.

   IT ANNOUNCES NOTHING: the control's own checked state is the report, and a region would say it twice.
   A browser that refuses to SAVE is a different matter and goes to the page through Refused.
   SourceContractTests.TheTileAndTheMarkControlsTakeNoAnnouncer pins the silence. *@
<fieldset class="rating">
    <legend>Your rating out of 10</legend>

    <div class="rating-options">
        <label class="rating-clear">
            <input type="radio" name="your-rating" checked="@(score is null)"
                   @onchange="() => ChooseAsync(null)" /> Not rated
        </label>

        @for (var value = TitlePreference.MinRating; value <= TitlePreference.MaxRating; value++)
        {
            var choice = value;
            <label>
                <input type="radio" name="your-rating" checked="@(score == choice)"
                       @onchange="() => ChooseAsync(choice)" /> @choice
            </label>
        }
    </div>
</fieldset>

@code {
    /// <summary>What the group is drawing, which is the saved score until a choice changes it.</summary>
    private int? score;

    /// <summary>Which half of the document this title is filed under.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public required TitleKind Kind { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The published key: <c>K</c> for a film, the slug for a series. Never the TMDB id.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public required string Key { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The record's TMDB id, saved beside the key so a re-keyed record can be found again.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int TmdbId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Called when the browser refused to save. The page renders <see cref="FavouriteButton.CouldNotSave"/>;
    /// the group has already put itself back to the score the browser actually holds.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback Refused { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Reads the saved score on every parameter set rather than once, for the reason
    /// <c>FavouriteButton.OnParametersSet</c> gives: a record page that routes from one title to another
    /// keeps its component instances, and a score read once would show the previous title's.
    /// </summary>
    protected override void OnParametersSet() => score = Preferences.RatingOf(Kind, Key);

    /// <summary>
    /// Records the score, drawing it before the write for the reason <c>FavouriteButton</c> records, and
    /// putting it back if the browser refuses.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Null is "Not rated" and it CLEARS the score rather than storing a zero. Zero is a real score here --
    /// the owner may think a film is worth nothing -- so the two cannot share a representation, and
    /// <c>PreferenceStoreTests.ZeroIsAScoreAndNotAnAbsence</c> pins that from the store's side.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task ChooseAsync(int? chosen)
    {
        var was = score;
        score = chosen;

        try
        {
            await Preferences.SetRatingAsync(Kind, Key, TmdbId, chosen);
        }
        catch (PreferenceStorageException)
        {
            score = was;
            await Refused.InvokeAsync();
        }
    }
}
